  "problem": "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{\u2032}(0)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{\u2032}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{\u2032}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{\u2032}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $4$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{\u2032}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(x)={g}^{\u2032}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(0)={g}^{\u2032}(0){f}^{-1}(0)+\\frac{g(0)}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(0))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(0)=-3,$ $g(0)=1,$ ${g}^{\u2032}(0)=\\frac{1}{3},$ and ${f}^{\u2032}(-3)=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(0) & ={g}^{\u2032}(0){f}^{-1}(0)+\\frac{g(0)}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-3)+\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}(-3)} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
